Document (dev.stxt.website): STXT vs Markdown — Complementary, not alternatives
	Metadata:
		Author: Claude Fable 5
		Revision: Joan Costa Mombiela
		Last modif: 2026-09-08
		Description: Markdown formats prose; STXT structures documents and adopts Markdown for the prose of its nodes. Two examples, and why MARKDOWN is the only embedded format in STXT schemas.

	Header: @STXT@ vs Markdown

	Assert >>
		Markdown gives format to prose; @STXT@ gives structure to documents.\\
		They solve different problems, and @STXT@ adopts Markdown for the prose of its documents.

	Content >>
		Markdown answers "how do I write bold, links and lists inside a text?"; @STXT@
		answers "what are the parts of this document, and are they all there?". A README
		needs the first; a contract, an article pipeline or a decision log needs both.

	Subheader: The data of a Markdown document

	Content >>
		Markdown has no notion of fields. When a document needs data —author, date,
		status—, the convention is **front matter**: a YAML block between `---` fences,
		foreign to Markdown and parsed by a second language with
		[its own rules](stxt-vs-yaml):

	Listing >>
		---
		title: Style guide
		author: Ana López
		published: 2026-09-01
		---

		# Style guide

		Write **short** sentences. Prefer [links](https://example.com) to footnotes.

	Content >>
		Nothing in that file checks that `published` is a date, that `author` is present
		or that the body has the agreed sections. In @STXT@ the record is nodes and the
		prose is a block whose type declares it as Markdown:

	Code >>
		Article (com.example.article): Style guide
			Author: Ana López
			Published: 2026-09-01
			Body >>
				Write **short** sentences. Prefer [links](https://example.com) to footnotes.

	Content >>
		And the template for its namespace:

	Code >>
		Template (@stxt.template): com.example.article
			Structure >>
				Article (com.example.article):
					Author: (+)
					Published: (1) DATE
					Body: (1) MARKDOWN

	Content >>
		`MARKDOWN` is the only embedded format the @STXT@ schema layer defines: it marks
		the text of a node as formatted prose, to be interpreted as CommonMark, and its
		raw form stays readable ([STXT-SCHEMA-SPEC §9.7](stxt-schema-ref#s9-7)). The
		validator checks what the front matter only suggested: the author is there, the
		date is a date and the body is exactly one block.

	Subheader: Structure by heading convention

	Content >>
		When a Markdown document has fixed parts, the parts are headings, and the
		agreement on which they are and what they contain lives outside the file. An
		architecture decision record (ADR) in the usual layout:

	Listing >>
		# ADR 12: Adopt PostgreSQL

		## Status

		Accepted

		## Context

		The current store has no transactions across collections, and the
		billing module needs them.

		## Decision

		Migrate the billing data to PostgreSQL 16 during Q4.

	Content >>
		A reader knows that `Status` takes one of four values and that `Consequences`
		should exist; no Markdown tool can check it, because to Markdown everything is
		headings and paragraphs. In @STXT@ the parts are nodes, and the prose of each one
		is still Markdown:

	Code >>
		ADR (com.example.adr): Adopt PostgreSQL
			Number: 12
			Status: Accepted
			Context >>
				The current store has no transactions across collections, and the
				billing module needs them.
			Decision >>
				Migrate the billing data to PostgreSQL 16 during Q4.
			Consequences >>
				The two services share one database until the migration ends.

	Content >>
		And the template that fixes the agreement:

	Code >>
		Template (@stxt.template): com.example.adr
			Structure >>
				ADR (com.example.adr):
					Number: (1) NATURAL
					Status: (1) ENUM [Proposed, Accepted, Deprecated, Superseded]
					Context: (1) MARKDOWN
					Decision: (1) MARKDOWN
					Consequences: (1) MARKDOWN

	Content >>
		A status outside the list or a missing section is a validation error, not a
		pending review:

	Code >>
		# ERROR: Status must be one of Proposed, Accepted, Deprecated, Superseded
		ADR (com.example.adr): Adopt PostgreSQL
			Number: 12
			Status: Approved
			Context >>
				The current store has no transactions across collections.
			Decision >>
				Migrate the billing data to PostgreSQL 16 during Q4.
			Consequences >>
				The two services share one database until the migration ends.

	Subheader: This site

	Content >>
		Every page of `stxt.dev`, this one included, is an @STXT@ document whose prose
		blocks are Markdown, validated against the site template before rendering
		([CMS and publishing](use-cases-cms)). Add `.stxt` to the address of any page to
		read the source.

	Subheader: When to use each

	Content >>
		For a README, a comment or a gist —lightly formatted prose with no contract—,
		Markdown alone.

		@STXT@, when the document has named parts that must be checked:
		[articles in a pipeline](use-cases-cms),
		[corporate records](use-cases-corporate-docs),
		[technical proposals](use-cases-rfcs). The prose inside is still Markdown; the
		structure around it becomes explicit and validated. The short version of this
		page is in the [FAQ](faq#why-not-markdown).
